    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_127777bde46e569d164e0f48e0f90940f83a21ce592ea4826903c5ec2cd0bd65",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Positivity lets two aggregate pair estimates yield different arithmetic conclusions. For simplicity, equal-ordinate pairs are the multiplicity second moment and are dominated by a nonnegative squared-sinc pair sum, after which comparison with the linear multiplicity count forces a positive proportion of simple zeros. For gaps, a triangular difference kernel with nonnegative transform gives a lower bound whose diagonal subtraction leaves a positive off-diagonal certificate C(A), forcing a gap below the mean scale.",
