    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_557847483cc448115670acf14e4f5a3a8c906f48b5bcfa3d7f44db2857f77bff",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The vicinity of unit frequency is a common boundary in three representations. A dilation test contradicts uniform continuation of the low-frequency output law; in the proof, x=T^alpha reaches the scale where earlier errors and nondiagonal terms cease to be negligible; regrouping those terms exposes shifted von Mangoldt correlations. A character-average analogue gives limited structural support for stability beyond that boundary, but does not prove the zeta claim.",
